% ============================================================================
% speleothem-substrate — what a speleothem grows ON (adj-facts-stdlib,
% EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? speleothem_substrate(helictite, $S)
%       % cave_ceiling
%       % cave_wall
%       % cave_floor_less_often
%       % carbonate_coating
%       % crust
%       % soda_straw_sometimes
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `speleothem_substrate(speleothem, substrate)` carrying the
% source citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query.
% The query also runs BACKWARD — "what grows on cave walls?" is a question
% nothing in this stdlib could answer before.
%
% *** THIS IS A DIFFERENT RELATION FROM `speleothem_growth_surface`, AND
% THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE REASON IT EXISTS. ***
%
% `speleothem-growth-surface.adj` answers "the surface a dripstone grows
% FROM", and is single-valued: a stalactite hangs from the ceiling, a
% stalagmite builds up from the floor, one surface each. It deliberately
% ABSTAINED on `helictite`, and its header gives the reason: the source
% places helictites on three surfaces with a frequency hedge on the third,
% and "one surface would drop the others while three-as-equals would
% flatten the source's own frequency hedge."
%
% That reasoning was right about THAT relation. It is not an argument that
% the fact is untableable — only that it does not fit a single-valued
% "grows from" relation. This relation is multi-valued and means "grows
% ON", which is the source's own verb for these speleothems, and it carries
% the hedge the ordinary way: INSIDE THE ATOM of the value the hedge
% modifies, following the placement rule `veto-override.adj` and
% `karst-process-zone.adj` already apply. So `cave_floor_less_often` is a
% value and bare `cave_floor` is NOT — a query for the unhedged floor
% abstains, because asking for it is asking where helictites grow just as
% readily as anywhere else, which this source declines to say.
%
% Same move `speleothem-component.adj` made for the `column` abstention:
% when a table declines, the fix is a new relation shaped to the question,
% never a loosened row in the old one.
%
% TWO SPELEOTHEMS, NOT ONE. This was nearly built as a helictite-only
% table, which would have been thin enough to question. The page states the
% same shape twice — a list of substrates ending in a hedged floor — so
% `frostwork` is a second member and the relation is a real axis rather
% than a wrapper around one sentence.
%
% Provenance: three sentences from the U.S. National Park Service's
% "Speleothems" page.
%
%   - The `source` envelope quotes TWO CONTIGUOUS SENTENCES as one string.
%     That is deliberate. The second begins "They typically grow on other
%     speleothems…", and cited alone its subject is a bare pronoun — a
%     reader could not tell who "They" refers to. The two sentences are
%     adjacent in the article, so quoting the pair is still verbatim and
%     makes the citation self-contained. A citation that cannot be read
%     without the page open is not doing its job.
%   - The `cites` corroboration carries the frostwork sentence.
%
% HEDGE PLACEMENT, three qualifiers, each placed by the settled rule:
%
%     qualifier         modifies            therefore lives
%     ---------------   -----------------   -----------------------------
%     "less often"      cave floors only    in the atom
%                                           (cave_floor_less_often)
%     "sometimes"       soda straws only    in the atom
%                                           (soda_straw_sometimes)
%     "typically"       the whole clause    in the CITATION, which quotes
%                       "grow on other      it verbatim; the atoms
%                       speleothems"        carbonate_coating and crust
%                                           stay bare
%     "less             frostwork's         in the atom, with FROSTWORK'S
%      occasionally"    floors only         OWN WORDING preserved
%                                           (cave_floor_less_occasionally)
%
% Note the last one. Frostwork's hedge is NOT normalised to match
% helictite's, even though "less occasionally" is awkward English and
% plainly means the same thing. They are different sentences and each atom
% carries the word its own sentence used. Smoothing them into one spelling
% would be editing a citation to make a table tidier.
%
% TWO THINGS THIS TABLE DOES NOT CLAIM, stated so nobody reads them in:
%
%   - THE SUBSTRATE COLUMN MIXES KINDS, on purpose. Some values are parts
%     of the cave (`cave_ceiling`, `cave_wall`, `ledge`) and some are other
%     speleothems (`stalactite`, `carbonate_coating`, `crust`,
%     `soda_straw_sometimes`). The relation means "the thing it grows on",
%     which is coherent across both, and the source itself mixes them in a
%     single list ("on stalactites, walls, ceilings, ledges"). Do not read
%     a value as necessarily being a cave surface.
%   - THE LISTS ARE NOT CLOSED. The helictite substrate sentence says "SUCH
%     AS carbonate coatings, crusts" — exemplary, not exhaustive. Absence
%     of a substrate from this table means the source did not name it, NOT
%     that a speleothem does not grow there.
%
% `trust authoritative` — the National Park Service is a first-party .gov
% publisher, the same source the seven sibling cave/karst libraries cite.
% Every sentence was taken from a RAW TEXT EXTRACTION of the page, never
% from a fetch summary; summarised readings of this same page have
% disagreed with each other about what it contains.
%
% Provenance is TABLE-level, so all eleven rows carry both sentences and
% `--explain` shows only the primary one — meaning the frostwork rows
% display under the helictite sentence there. That is a property of every
% multi-sentence table in this stdlib, tracked as issue #13898; the JSON
% output is the authoritative view.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - Bare `cave_floor` is NOT a value, for either speleothem. Both
%     sentences hedge the floor and the hedge rides in the atom, so the
%     unqualified query correctly finds nothing.
%   - `stalagmite`, `column`, `drapery` and the rest get no rows. This page
%     describes what they are made of and where they form, but never says
%     what they grow ON, and inferring a substrate from general karst
%     knowledge is exactly what a grounded recall library must not do.
%   - `slanted_surface` is not a row. Cave bacon "forms on slanted
%     surfaces", but that is a description of the SHAPE of a surface rather
%     than an identifiable thing in a cave, and it would not join with any
%     other value in this column.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table speleothem_substrate {
    columns speleothem, substrate

    row (helictite, cave_ceiling)
    row (helictite, cave_wall)
    row (helictite, cave_floor_less_often)
    row (helictite, carbonate_coating)
    row (helictite, crust)
    row (helictite, soda_straw_sometimes)

    row (frostwork, stalactite)
    row (frostwork, cave_wall)
    row (frostwork, cave_ceiling)
    row (frostwork, ledge)
    row (frostwork, cave_floor_less_occasionally)

    source "Helictites grow on cave ceilings, walls, and less often on cave floors. They typically grow on other speleothems, such as carbonate coatings, crusts, and sometimes on soda straws."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
    trust authoritative
    cites "Frostwork can also be found on stalactites, walls, ceilings, ledges, and less occasionally on floors (Hill, 1997)." locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
}
